:title("PHP (subset) to LLVM IR compiler") ;
:description("Compiles a PHP subset to an LLVM IR module and runs it with the built-in
IR interpreter and the MetaJS handle runtime (llvm.RunJS).

A file starts in HTML MODE: text outside '<?php ... ?>' is literal output (one print
of a constant string), '?>' also stands in for a missing ';', the newline right after
a close tag is swallowed and '<?= e ?>' is shorthand for echo.

The subset matches php-interpreter.abnf: the '<?php' open tag, dollar-sigil variables
(create on first assignment, plus += -= *= /= %= .=), integers, double/single quoted
strings with '$var' / '{$expr}' interpolation, true/false/null, arithmetic and comparison,
the '.' concatenation operator, && || ! and the words and/or, the ternary, prefix/postfix
++ --, if/elseif/else, while, for (;;), foreach ($a as $v) / ($a as $k => $v), functions
with return and DEFAULT parameter values, arrow functions fn($x) => e, array
destructuring (nested, keyed and with holes, in an assignment and in foreach),
closures function($x) use ($y) {...}, class C { public function m() {...} }
with $this->prop / ->method() / new C(...), indexed and associative arrays ([...] and
array(...)), $a[] append, PHP's array and string library (lib/php-stdlib.php, written in
PHP on a handful of natives), and echo/print.

PHP's VALUE MODEL is the one php-interpreter.abnf documents, and the two must agree:
an int is 64 bit two's complement (a plain number while it stays exact, a box beyond
2^53) whose overflow PROMOTES to a float rather than wrapping, a float is BOXED (so 3.0
is not identical to 3 and match(3.0) picks the float arm) and prints through two
renderers (precision=14 for echo, serialize_precision=-1 for var_dump), a string is
byte based, and an array is ONE ordered key/value map
with a next-automatic-index, so === compares the keys and their order while == does not.
Truthiness is PHP's (false, 0, 0.0, the empty string, the string zero, the empty array
and null are falsy), == is PHP 8 loose equality and the whole operator ladder, the casts
and the library natives (count, strlen, substr, strpos, ...) go through the js_ph* externals
(abnf/jsrtphp.go, abnf/jsrtphpstr.go),
each the twin of the like-named function in the interpreter.

Classes carry class CONSTANTS and STATIC properties in two prefixed key spaces on the
descriptor, both resolved by walking the inheritance chain - so a subclass shares its
parent's single static storage. self:: and static:: live in the CALL FRAME, not in
globals, so the scope-chain snapshot a try restores covers them; static:: is the runtime
class of this, or the class a C::m() call named (late static binding).

Locals, functions, methods and closures lower to the MetaJS handle runtime: scopes via
js_scope_new, a variable read and written in its own frame only via js_scope_own /
js_scope_decl (PHP has no lexical variable scope), closures via js_closure
+ js_call, objects/properties via js_obj_new / js_get / js_set, method dispatch via
js_phmcall (instances carry a __class descriptor; new C() runs __construct with the
instance bound to $this; php's own name, because a method nothing claims must raise
PHP's catchable Error and the shared js_mcall may not be overridden - manual ch.4), arrays via js_pharr / js_phset / js_phpush, indexing via js_phget,
foreach via js_range_len / js_range_key / js_range_val over that same ordered map.
'.' concatenation coerces via js_phcat; && || coerce to booleans with js_phbool. The whole program becomes phpmain(env,
args); a self checking program ends with exit(failures). The same subset is interpreted
directly by php-interpreter.abnf, and the two must agree.") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib): identifiers, integers, booleans.
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// PHP whitespace: blanks plus '#' and '//' line comments and '/* */' block comments.
Whitespace  = { @+" \t\r\n" | LineComment | BlockComment } ;
// '#' starts a line comment, but '#[' opens an ATTRIBUTE - the negative lookahead
// keeps the attribute out of the comment.
LineComment = ( "//" | HashOnly ) :whitespace() { NotNewline } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
HashOnly    = "#" !"[" ;
BlockComment = "/*" :whitespace() { NotStar | "*" { "*" } NotStarSlash } "*" { "*" } "/" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
NotNewline  = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStar     = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStarSlash = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\x2e" | "\x30"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// The whole program. A PHP file starts in HTML mode: everything before the first
// '<?php' is literal output, '?>' switches back to HTML mode, and the file may
// alternate any number of times (a file with no '<?php' at all is pure output).
Program     <~~ buildMain(takeAll()) ~~>
            = LeadHtml { TopItem } ;
// One item of a top level: a declaration or a statement. Factored out because a
// BRACED namespace body holds exactly the same items (see NsDecl).
// A top-level DECLARATION never reaches the Statement rule (the same names are
// alternatives there, for a declaration inside a body), so it takes the same
// source-position wrapper here rather than none at all. Statement stays out of
// this group: it carries its own marker, and two markers for one statement would
// report it twice.
TopItem     = HaltTail | TopDecl | Statement ;
// __halt_compiler(); ends the program text. Twin of HaltTail in php-interpreter.abnf.
HaltTail    = ( "__halt_compiler" | "__HALT_COMPILER" ) "(" ")" ( ";" | "?>" )
              :whitespace() { "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
TopDecl     <~~ push(phStmtPos(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "" <~~ push(up.pos) ~~>
              ( Def <~~ push(makeTopDef(pop())) ~~> | ClassDecl <~~ push(makeTopClass(pop())) ~~> | EnumDecl | ConstDecl
              | TypeDecl <~~ push(makeTopClass(pop())) ~~> | NsDecl | UseDecl ) ;
OpenTag     = "<?" :whitespace() CiP CiH CiP :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// Text before the first open tag. Unlike the '?>' form it keeps a leading newline:
// only the newline directly after a CLOSE tag is swallowed.
LeadHtml    <~~ push(makeHtmlOut(pop(), false)) ~~>
            = HtmlText [ OpenTag ] ;
// '?>' leaves PHP mode. Twin of InlineHtml in php-interpreter.abnf; see there.
InlineHtml  <~~ push(makeHtmlOut(pop(), true)) ~~>
            = "?>" HtmlText [ OpenTag ] ;
// The raw run itself. Whitespace skipping is turned OFF around the scan: the leading
// blanks and newlines of the run are OUTPUT, so they must not be eaten as separators
// before the token the scanner returns is matched.
HtmlText    <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = :whitespace() HtmlScan :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
HtmlScan = :script(~~
    (function() {
        // Everything from here to the next opening tag is literal text. '<?php' is
        // matched case-insensitively (PHP's tag is), and '<?=' is the echo shorthand,
        // which ShortEcho then parses as an echo statement.
        var end = 0
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 100000000) {
            steps = steps + 1
            var b = c.peek(end)
            if (b < 0) { break }
            if (b === 60 && c.peek(end + 1) === 63) {
                if (c.peek(end + 2) === 61) { break }
                var p1 = c.peek(end + 2)
                var h1 = c.peek(end + 3)
                var p2 = c.peek(end + 4)
                if ((p1 === 112 || p1 === 80) && (h1 === 104 || h1 === 72) && (p2 === 112 || p2 === 80)) { break }
            }
            end = end + 1
        }
        // An empty run must SUCCEED matching nothing; returning a zero-length token
        // would not. undefined is the dialect's "match empty".
        if (end === 0) { return undefined }
        // c.peek answers BYTES, so the UTF-8 has to be decoded here or a run holding
        // any non-ASCII text would never match back against the source.
        var out = ""
        var p = 0
        while (p < end) {
            var b0 = c.peek(p)
            if (b0 < 128) { out = out + String.fromCharCode(b0); p = p + 1; continue }
            if (b0 >= 240) {
                var cp4 = ((b0 - 240) * 262144) + ((c.peek(p + 1) - 128) * 4096) + ((c.peek(p + 2) - 128) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 3) - 128)
                var off = cp4 - 65536
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(55296 + ((off - (off % 1024)) / 1024)) + String.fromCharCode(56320 + (off % 1024))
                p = p + 4
                continue
            }
            if (b0 >= 224) {
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(((b0 - 224) * 4096) + ((c.peek(p + 1) - 128) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 2) - 128))
                p = p + 3
                continue
            }
            if (b0 >= 192) {
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(((b0 - 192) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 1) - 128))
                p = p + 2
                continue
            }
            return abnf.newToken("\x01badutf8", 0)
        }
        return abnf.newToken(out, 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// '<?= e, e ?>' is shorthand for 'echo e, e;'.
ShortEcho   <~~ push(makeEcho(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ShortOpen <~~ push({cmd: "echo"}) ~~> [ Expression { "," Expression } ] [ ";" ] ;
ShortOpen   = "<?=" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A statement terminator. PHP lets a close tag stand in for the ';' ("echo 1 ?>"),
// so every statement that ends in ';' accepts a '?>' AHEAD of it instead - the tag
// itself is left in place and read as the InlineHtml statement that follows.
Semi        = ";" | CloseAhead ;
CloseAhead  = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 100000) {
            steps = steps + 1
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i); continue }
            break
        }
        if (cc === 63 && c.peek(i + 1) === 62) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01noclosetag", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// A function definition: function name($a, $b) { ... } with explicit return. An
// optional parameter type hint and return type are accepted and ignored (dynamic).
// 'function &name()' returns by REFERENCE (DefAmp, refFnEnter).
Def         <~~ push(makeFun(takeAll())); refFnStack.pop() ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwFunction [ DefAmp ] Name <~~ push(noteFnName(up.in)); refFnEnter(up.in); fnEnter(); cloEnterFn(up.in, false) ~~> ParamList [ ReturnType ] Block ;
DefAmp      <~~ defAmp = true ~~> = "&" ;
ParamList   = "(" [ Param { "," Param } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
// A parameter accepts real-world surface: promotion modifiers (public/readonly...),
// a type hint, by-reference '&', variadic '...', and a default value. All but the
// name are accepted and dropped (dynamic, create-on-assign); the default expression
// is parsed then popped so only {param} reaches makeFun.
// A modifier on a constructor parameter is PHP 8's property PROMOTION
// ('__construct(public int $x)' declares and assigns $this->x), so it is captured
// rather than skipped; makeFun turns each promoted parameter into one prologue
// assignment. On any other function a modifier is meaningless and simply ignored.
Param       = [ AttrGrp ] { ParamMod } [ TypeHint ] [ ParamRef ] [ ParamRest ] DollarName <~~ push({param: pop()}) ~~> [ ParamDefault ] ;
// 'function f(&$n)': the argument is passed by REFERENCE. The callee simply keeps the
// cell in its parameter slot (every read dereferences, every write writes through);
// it is the CALL SITE that has to hand a cell over, which is why makeDefStmt records
// each function's by-reference positions for makeCall to read back.
ParamRef    <~~ noteRef(); push({pref: true}) ~~> = "&" ;
// 'function f(...$rest)': every remaining argument collected into one PHP array.
ParamRest   <~~ push({prest: true}) ~~> = "..." ;
ParamMod    <~~ push({pmod: up.in}) ~~> = Modifier ;
// A default value is kept: it becomes a small prologue in the emitted body that
// substitutes the default when the argument was not passed (see paramDefaults).
ParamDefault <~~ push({pdef: pop()}) ~~> = PlainEq Expression ;
TypeHint    = [ "?" ] TypeAtom { ( "|" | "&" ) [ "?" ] TypeAtom } ;
// A DNF type (PHP 8.2) parenthesizes an INTERSECTION inside a union: '(X&Y)|Z',
// 'X|(A&B)'. The parenthesized group is just another atom of the surrounding
// TypeHint, so one alternative covers every position a type may stand in (parameter,
// return, property). Type hints carry no runtime meaning in this subset.
TypeAtom    = KwArray | TypeParen | [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } ;
TypeParen   = "(" TypeHint ")" ;
ReturnType  = ":" TypeHint ;

// class C { public function m() {...} } -- method modifiers are accepted and ignored.
ClassDecl   <~~ push(makeClassStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { ClassMod } KwClass Name <~~ push(cloNoteClass(up.in)) ~~> [ KwExtends ExtName <~~ push({ext: up.in}) ~~> ] [ KwImplements ImplNames ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// The implements list of a real class declaration, captured by name (ImplList itself
// stays item-neutral: interface/trait/enum/anonymous-class all reference it and pop a
// fixed number of items).
ImplNames   = ImplName { "," ImplName } ;
ImplName    <~~ push({impl: up.in}) ~~> = ExtName ;
ClassMod    = ( CiAbstract | CiFinal | CiReadonly ) KwEnd ;
CiAbstract  = CiA :whitespace() CiB CiS CiT CiR CiA CiC CiT :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CiFinal     = CiF :whitespace() CiI CiN CiA CiL :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CiReadonly  = CiR :whitespace() CiE CiA CiD CiO CiN CiL CiY :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ExtName     = [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } ;
Member      = MethodDecl | ClassConst | UseTrait | PropDecl ;
// A method: of its modifiers only the visibility is kept, and a reference-return
// '&' is accepted and ignored; an abstract/interface method has no body (';'
// instead of a block -> empty method).
MethodDecl  <~~ push({method: makeFun(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { MethMod } KwFunction [ "&" ] MethodName <~~ push(noteFnName(up.in)); fnEnter(); cloEnterFn(up.in, true) ~~> ParamList [ ReturnType ] ( Block | ";" ) ;
// Class constants: 'const A = 1, B = 2;', with PHP 8.3's typed form ('const int
// SIZE = 4') and any modifier (final/public/...). They are published on the class
// descriptor under a 'k:' key and resolved by walking __super, so a subclass and an
// implementing class inherit them. An initializer may read 'self::' - makeClassStmt
// binds the class as self BEFORE evaluating them, in declaration order, which is what
// makes 'const TWICE = self::SIZE * 2' work.
// The TYPE of a PHP 8.3 typed class constant is a full TypeHint, not just a word:
// 'const ?string C1', 'const array|string C10', 'const stdClass&Stringable C' all
// occur in php-src's own suite. The typed form has to be its own alternative and be
// tried FIRST - writing it as an optional [ TypeHint ] would let 'const SIZE = 4'
// consume SIZE as the type and then fail on '=', because a PEG never retries an
// optional that matched. CIds also covers the plain 'const int SIZE' spelling, so
// both routes reach makeCItem with the same {cname} records.
ClassConst  <~~ push({cconsts: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { Modifier } KwConst ( ConstTyped | ConstPlain ) ";" ;
ConstTyped  = TypeHint CItem { "," CItem } ;
ConstPlain  = CItem { "," CItem } ;
CItem       <~~ push(makeCItem(takeAll())) ~~>
            = CIds [ "=" Expression ] ;
// 'const int SIZE' is a TYPED constant: the last word is the name. Each word pushes
// its own record - up.in of the whole run would concatenate them without a separator
// ("intSIZE"), since the capture drops the skipped whitespace.
CIds        = CWord { CWord } ;
CWord       <~~ push({cname: up.in}) ~~> = Id ;
// use T1, T2 { ... } inside a class body: the members of each trait are FLATTENED
// into the class. The adaptation block resolves the conflicts a flat copy would
// create: 'A::m insteadof B' drops B's m, and 'T::m as alias' adds a second name for
// one (an optional visibility word in front is accepted and ignored).
UseTrait    <~~ push(makeUseTrait(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwUse ImplNames ( ";" | TraitAdapt ) ;
TraitAdapt  = "{" { TraitRule } "}" ;
TraitRule   = TraitInsteadof | TraitAs ;
TraitInsteadof <~~ push(makeInsteadof(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TraitRef KwInsteadof ImplNames ";" ;
TraitAs     <~~ push(makeTraitAs(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TraitRef KwAs [ TraitVis ] [ TraitAlias ] ";" ;
TraitRef    <~~ push({tref: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Id [ "::" Id ] ;
TraitAlias  <~~ push({alias: up.in}) ~~> = Id ;
TraitVis    <~~ push({tvis: up.in}) ~~> = ( "public" | "private" | "protected" ) KwEnd ;
KwInsteadof = CiI :whitespace() CiN CiS CiT CiE CiA CiD CiO CiF :whitespace(Whitespace) KwEnd ;

// interface I extends J, K { const C = 1; public function m(): T; }
// An interface becomes a class descriptor with no method bodies: it carries its
// constants and its parent interfaces, which is exactly what instanceof, a typed
// catch and 'I::CONST' need. A trait is the other half of TypeDecl and IS flattened
// into every using class - see TraitDecl and UseTrait below.
TypeDecl    = IfaceDecl | TraitDecl ;
IfaceDecl   <~~ push(makeIfaceStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwInterface Name <~~ push(cloNoteClass(up.in)) ~~> [ KwExtends ImplNames ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// trait T { ... }: a compile-time bag of members, with no descriptor of its own -
// PHP traits are a copy/paste unit, not a type - so the declaration emits nothing and
// only records its member list for the classes that 'use' it.
TraitDecl   <~~ push(makeTraitDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwTrait Name <~~ push(cloNoteClass(up.in)) ~~> "{" { Member } "}" ;
// namespace N; and the BRACED form namespace N { ... } (and the global 'namespace {}').
// Names are not qualified in this subset, so the declaration itself stays a no-op -
// but the braced body is a top level of its own and its items are simply emitted in
// place, which is what makes a file that wraps everything in 'namespace X { }' work.
NsDecl      <~~ push(makeNs(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwNamespace { Id | "\\" } ( Semi | NsBody ) ;
NsBody      <~~ push({nsbody: makeBody(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "{" { TopItem } "}" ;
// use A\B; / use A\B as C, D\E as F; / use function f; / use const C;
// and the GROUP form 'use Foo\Bar\{A, B as C, function f, const K,};' of PHP 7.
// Recognized whole and routed to notImplemented. Twin of UseDecl in
// php-interpreter.abnf; see there.
UseDecl     <~~ push(notImplStmt("use import", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwUse [ UseKind ] UseItem { "," UseItem } Semi ;
UseKind     = ( "function" | "const" ) KwEnd ;
UseItem     = UseGroup | UsePath ;
UseGroup    = [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } "\\" "{" UseInner { "," UseInner } [ "," ] "}" ;
UseInner    = [ UseKind ] UsePath ;
UsePath     = [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } [ KwAs Id ] ;
// enum Name : backing-type implements I { case A = "a"; const C = 1; method... }
// An enum is a CLASS whose cases are singleton instances published as class constants,
// so 'E::A' is one constant read, 'E::A === E::A' is object identity (which is what
// makes an enum usable as a match arm) and the methods, constants and interfaces are
// the ordinary class machinery. cases() answers them in declaration order.
EnumDecl    <~~ push(makeEnumStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwEnum Name <~~ push(cloNoteClass(up.in)) ~~> [ ":" TypeAtom ] [ KwImplements ImplNames ]
              "{" { EnumMember } "}" ;
EnumMember  = EnumCase | Member ;
EnumCase    <~~ push(makeEnumCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwCase Name <~~ push({ecase: up.in}) ~~> [ "=" Expression ] ";" ;
SkipDq      = '"' :whitespace() { SkipStrEsc | SkipDqChar } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipSq      = "'" :whitespace() { SkipStrEsc | SkipSqChar } "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipStrEsc  = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipDqChar  = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipSqChar  = "\x00"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// An attribute group '#[Foo(1), Bar]': balanced-skipped wherever PHP allows one.
// Attributes carry no runtime meaning in this subset, so they are recognized and
// dropped rather than routed to notImplemented.
AttrGrp     = Attr { Attr } ;
Attr        = "#[" { AttrItem } "]" ;
AttrItem    = SkipDq | SkipSq | AttrBr | AttrOther ;
AttrBr      = "[" { AttrItem } "]" ;
AttrOther   = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5a" | "\x5c" | "\x5e"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// A property declaration 'public $x;' or 'public int $x = e;'. The type hint is
// dropped (dynamic), but the DEFAULT VALUE is kept: makeNew replays the declared
// defaults down the inheritance chain, so "$o->val" reads 7 before anything is
// assigned. A 'static' property is stored on the CLASS descriptor instead (under an
// 's:' key), and because js_phsset walks __super to the declaring class, a subclass
// shares its parent's single storage - PHP's rule.
// One declaration may introduce SEVERAL properties - 'public $name, $bars;' and
// 'static public $a, $aa;' both occur in php-src's own suite - so the tag pushes one
// {propdecl} per name rather than exactly one, and every name inherits the shared
// modifiers and type hint.
PropDecl    <~~ var pds = makeProps(takeAll()); for (var pi = 0; pi < pds.length; pi++) { push({propdecl: pds[pi]}) } ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } [ PropType ] DollarName <~~ cloNoteProp(up.in) ~~> [ "=" Expression ]
              { "," DollarName [ "=" Expression ] } ( ";" | PropHooks ) ;
// PHP 8.4 property hooks: 'public int $x = 1 { get => e; set { ... } }' (and the
// bodyless 'get;' / 'set;' of an abstract or interface property, which declares a hook
// without providing one). Each hook becomes an ordinary METHOD on the class -
// __hget_<prop> / __hset_<prop> - and a read or write of that property name dispatches
// to it, the same shape __get / __set already use. The property itself stays a
// declared property and IS the backing store: inside its own hook '$this->x' reads and
// writes that store directly instead of re-entering the hook, which is PHP's rule and
// the only thing that keeps a backed hook from recursing forever.
PropHooks   <~~ push({hooks: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "{" { PropHook } "}" ;
PropHook    <~~ push(makeHook(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } [ "&" ] HookName <~~ push({hname: cloEnterHook(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~>
              ( HookArrow | HookBlock | ";" ) ;
HookName    = ( "get" | "set" ) KwEnd ;
HookArrow   <~~ push({harrow: pop()}) ~~> = [ ParamList ] "=>" Expression ";" ;
HookBlock   <~~ push({hblock: pop().body}) ~~> = [ ParamList ] Block ;

PropMod     <~~ push({mod: up.in}) ~~> = Modifier ;
MethMod     <~~ push({mmod: up.in}) ~~> = Modifier ;
// A property's declared type. A typed property without a default starts
// UNINITIALISED - its slot holds undefined, not null - and var_dump names the type
// (uninitialized(?Foo\C)), so the text is kept.
PropType    <~~ push({ptype: propTypeText(up.in)}) ~~> = TypeHint ;

// 'private(set)' / 'protected(set)' is PHP 8.4 asymmetric visibility: the setter is
// more restricted than the getter. Only the plain visibility is enforced; the
// '(set)' suffix is accepted and leaves writes at that visibility.
Modifier    = ( "public" | "private" | "protected" | "static" | "final" | "abstract" | "readonly" ) KwEnd [ SetVis ] ;
SetVis      = "(" "set" ")" ;

// Body of a control structure / function: braces around a statement sequence.
Block       <~~ push({body: makeBody(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "{" { Statement } "}" ;
// A bare '{ ... }' statement: the block's body runs in place (PHP has no block scope).
BlockStmt   <~~ push(pop().body) ~~> = Block ;

// Every statement pushes exactly one thunk: function(block) -> block.
// A declaration is a statement too: PHP allows `if (...) { class Foo {} }`, and a
// conditionally declared class is common enough in real files to be worth parsing.
// A named 'function f() {}' written INSIDE a body is a declaration statement too (PHP
// registers it when control reaches it). DefStmt wraps Def so the thunk it pushes is
// the same makeDefStmt record TopItem builds; an ANONYMOUS function still rolls back
// here (Def demands a Name) and reaches ClosureExpr through ExprStmt.
// The empty-token tag ahead of the alternatives records where the statement STARTS,
// which is what the four position-reading tools want; phStmtPos says why up.pos at
// this level is not that position.
Statement   <~~ push(phStmtPos(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "" <~~ push(up.pos) ~~>
              ( InlineHtml | ShortEcho | EchoStmt | GlobalStmt | UnsetStmt | StaticVarStmt | BlockStmt | If | While | ForEach | For
            | Switch | DoWhile | Try | Throw | Declare
            | DefStmt | ClassDecl | EnumDecl | TypeDecl | ConstDecl | NsDecl | UseDecl
            | Return | Break | Continue | Goto | Label | Empty | ExprStmt ) ;
DefStmt     <~~ push(makeDefStmt(pop())) ~~> = Def ;
// goto L; / L: -- a jump inside one function body. The label is a real basic block, so
// the jump costs one branch; PHP's rule that a goto may LEAVE a block but never enter
